   "claim": "carried-into-wave-5.md requires the value-parity requests, including the repeated-slash case /a//b, to reach a handler, but does not say this case needs the mergeSlashes policy. Under the strict default policy no route can match /a//b: RoutePattern.parse rejects a pattern with an empty interior segment and a :variable never matches an empty segment, and Router.decodeSegments keeps the empty segment unless policy.mergeSlashes() is set. A wave-5 implementer following the note under the default policy gets 404 and has no handler-side rawPath()/query() to compare, so the getPath() '//'-merging hazard stays untested. Round 3 W2-005 asked for '/a//b under the default and the mergeSlashes policy'; the note dropped the policy qualifier.",
   "evidence": "docs/research/naruserve-v1/waves/carried-into-wave-5.md:3; NaruServe-api/src/main/java/com/snoworca/naruserve/routing/RoutePattern.java:49-50; NaruServe-api/src/main/java/com/snoworca/naruserve/routing/RoutePattern.java:101; NaruServe-api/src/main/java/com/snoworca/naruserve/routing/Router.java:296-300",
   "suggested_fix": "In carried-into-wave-5.md say that /a//b is sent with RouterPolicy.builder().mergeSlashes(true) (app.routerPolicy) to a /a/b route so it reaches the handler, and compare rawPath() (which must keep '//') across both adapters; under the strict default compare /a//b by status (404) only."
  }
